1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
The French Revolution is the central political event in modern history.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:05,880
You know, there are other things that compete.

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,880
The Russian Revolution, most obviously.

4
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:08,800
Or the American Revolution.

5
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:10,940
But I agree, neither of those really compare.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:11,720
No.

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,840
To me, the French Revolution is the paradigmatic example

8
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:17,280
of people trying to reboot a society,

9
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:19,600
to make it again, to make something new.

10
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,020
So it's that on the one hand.

11
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:24,420
And then, of course, there are other dimensions to it.

12
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:26,700
It becomes the great crucible of conservatism.

13
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:29,560
It's in response to the French Revolution that Edmund Burke,

14
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,620
writes the most famous example of kind of literary conservatism in history.

15
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:37,000
It's reflections on the revolution in France.

16
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:40,080
It's the French Revolution that exports the idea of the nation

17
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:44,180
and of modern nationalism across Europe and arguably the world.

18
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:47,140
And its iconography, Tom, we're still so familiar with it.

19
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:49,020
I mean, obviously, it gave France its national anthem.

20
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,740
It gave it its flag, the tricolour flag.

21
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:57,000
But the idea of left and right comes from the French Revolution,

22
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:58,360
from where people sat.

23
00:00:58,620 --> 00:00:59,980
In the National Assembly, right?

24
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:00,640
Yeah, exactly.

25
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,020
And when you think about all the great writing that's been done on it,

26
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:04,920
even just in English,

27
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:07,400
Dickens' A Tale of Two Cities,

28
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:11,960
one of the most influential books on our perception of history

29
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:12,980
that has ever been written.

30
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:14,600
You know, we mentioned Burke.

31
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:17,580
Dickens' influence by Carlyle and his great history of the revolution.

32
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,860
And of all the history books that have been written in our lifetimes,

33
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,180
one of the most influential on the way people write history,

34
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:27,480
I think, is Simon Sharma's book Citizens.

35
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,820
Some listeners may not agree with,

36
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,480
but Simon Sharma's take on the revolution,

37
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:35,520
his take is actually not dissimilar from Margaret Thatcher's

38
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:39,060
because he very much emphasises the violence and the costs of the revolution.

39
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,180
But the way he did it, the emphasis on narrative

40
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:48,580
and his ability to take unexpected aspects

41
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:53,020
and to take byroads and to make them so richly fascinating and colourful.

42
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:55,420
That's his great genius as a writer, isn't it?

43
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:58,800
Yeah, but that had an enormous impact on other historians.

44
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,620
I mean, it's a reflection of the inherent drama

45
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:05,160
and the scale and the sweep of the French Revolution

46
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,340
that it has inspired such great writers.

47
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:08,680
So there's Hilary Mantel as well.

48
00:02:09,540 --> 00:02:11,540
You know, her first great historical novel

49
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:12,520
that's about the French Revolution.

50
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:14,880
Actually, it's for me, her best book.

51
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:16,680
Well, one of her, certainly her best historical book.

52
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:18,940
No, I think it's...

53
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:21,600
And what makes it so interesting is not just the high drama,

54
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:22,780
but obviously the questions.

55
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:24,380
Why did it all happen?

56
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:26,500
And even more than that, what does it mean?

57
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:27,320
Well, Dominic, we've answered that.

58
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:28,700
It's because...

59
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:30,020
Because of the diamond necklace scandal.

60
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:32,360
Right, so we can stop right now.

61
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:33,260
Yeah, we've solved it.

62
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,380
So to be very, very simplistic for a second,

63
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:40,220
if people are only going to listen to six minutes of this podcast

64
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:42,500
and they want to know why the French Revolution happened,

65
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:44,360
I think there are three ingredients.

66
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:48,180
One is there is a financial and a high political crisis.

67
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:49,900
Effectively, France is running out of money.

68
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:52,840
Number two, a social crisis.

69
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:57,600
So there's horrendous weather, freak weather.

70
00:02:58,160 --> 00:02:58,600
There's...

71
00:02:58,600 --> 00:02:59,140
There's famine.

72
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,140
There is kind of mass hunger in France.

73
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:02,220
So contingency then.

74
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:02,980
So contingency.

75
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:03,780
Playing a very important role.

76
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,140
And then the third thing, which is much more deterministic,

77
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:07,880
is a kind of cultural moment.

78
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:10,520
And I know you'll be talking about this in a subsequent episode.

79
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:15,620
An obsession with progress, with purity,

80
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:17,340
and above all, with virtue.

81
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:22,040
And an idea that you can turn the world off and back on again

82
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:23,980
and start again with a clean slate.

83
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:24,980
Forget about that.

84
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:26,560
Yeah, forget about the record of history.

85
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:28,380
And I think it is the combination...

86
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:30,720
The combination of those three things that makes it different

87
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:33,380
from the two obvious antecedents, which are...

88
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:39,480
Mrs. Thatcher mentioned the Glorious Revolution in Britain in 1688, 1689.

89
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:42,620
And then, of course, the revolution in the American colonies.

90
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:44,300
Although that is important, I think.

91
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:48,840
It is important, but this is qualitatively different.

92
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:49,740
Yes.

93
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:52,040
So the question is, why did those three things...

94
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:53,660
How and why did they come together?

95
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:57,720
And for many years, historians looked for big overarching ideological explanations.

96
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:58,360
But then,

97
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:01,100
really, especially from the 1980s onwards,

98
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:04,440
and thanks partly to people like Sharma or Robert Darnton,

99
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:06,360
another brilliant historian, American historian,

100
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:07,460
who has written about this period,

101
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:10,740
historians started to use the French Revolution

102
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:14,540
as the subject to reinvigorate the idea of narrative,

103
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:16,820
to say, actually, events really matter.

104
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:21,840
And the chronology, the series, the chain is enormously important.

105
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:24,300
But you ended last time,

106
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:27,440
you did that brilliant episode about the Diamond Necklace scandal.

107
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:32,920
And your point that all the discussion about Marie Antoinette

108
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:36,440
and her, the sort of the semi-pornographic,

109
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:41,300
well, I mean, very literally pornographic pamphlets and the abuse of her,

110
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:49,000
that reflected an idea that if, as it were, the body of the queen or the king were diseased,

111
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,260
that reflected a disease in the body politic.

112
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:52,740
And that's how we ended.

113
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:55,860
But we didn't say why the body politic was diseased.

114
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:56,800
So,

115
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:58,560
what is wrong with...

116
00:04:58,560 --> 00:04:58,840
France?

117
00:04:58,900 --> 00:04:59,840
I guess that is the question.

118
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:01,820
And to start with,

119
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:05,160
let's kick off with the man that you introduced us to, Louis XVI.

120
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:08,180
So you described him as, I think, very reasonably,

121
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:11,020
a kind of reasonably likeable,

122
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:12,940
but ultimately slightly inconsequential man,

123
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:14,100
mild, affable,

124
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:16,280
loves his locks, going hunting,

125
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:18,940
but not a man it is easy to hate.

126
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:22,120
So you look at Louis XVI and you don't see a man

127
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:25,920
who millions of people would want to see dragged...

128
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:26,440
He's not a monster.

129
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:28,100
No, he's not a monster at all.

130
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:32,580
He became king when he was 19 years old in 1774.

131
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:34,900
And actually, that's, you might say, a young man,

132
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,980
but actually, that's quite old for a French king.

133
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:38,920
Yeah, because usually they're toddlers, aren't they?

134
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:42,620
His grandfather, Louis XV, had been five.

135
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:48,340
His predecessor, Louis XIV, had been four when he became king.

136
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:52,180
So actually, Louis is not badly placed and he's been prepared for it.

137
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:57,960
His kingdom, as we discussed before, the biggest kingdom in continental Europe,

138
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:58,980
28 million people,

139
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:02,560
the world's second largest economic power, really, after Britain,

140
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:07,600
the only European country that has both the serious navy and a serious army.

141
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:10,320
Britain, for example, only really has a navy.

142
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:14,520
Paris, very different from today's Paris.

143
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:17,100
So it's the second biggest city in Europe after London,

144
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:21,380
but it's a city, it's not the kind of grand city of avenues and boulevards.

145
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,400
Yeah, because they will come in later than the 19th century.

146
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:27,680
It's a city of slums, but also, you know, it's typically the urban poor and prostitutes

147
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:32,260
and criminals, but also a city of coffeehouses and books and ideas

148
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:36,480
and newspapers and theatres and all these kinds of things.

149
00:06:36,820 --> 00:06:40,280
And then beyond it, you have the other big cities of France also doing very well.

150
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:44,720
So France is unusual in having so many kind of provincial centres,

151
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:47,920
prosperous provincial centres, like Bordeaux and Lyon and stuff.

152
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:51,760
And then beyond that, eight out of ten French people work on the land.

153
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:55,300
In la France profonde, they are peasants, by and large.

154
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:57,120
So France is much, much more.

155
00:06:57,940 --> 00:06:59,040
Agricultural than Britain is.

156
00:06:59,180 --> 00:07:03,500
It is, and also much more kind of regionally distinct, really.

157
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:03,920
Yes.

158
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:06,480
Places like Brittany are very distinctive.

159
00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:08,740
And you saw that when we were talking about the Cardinal de Rohan,

160
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:13,320
that he has a sense of his distinctiveness because he is Breton.

161
00:07:13,620 --> 00:07:14,720
Yeah, absolutely.

162
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:19,160
So most, the funny thing about Louis XVI, Louis XVI would not have been able to understand

163
00:07:19,160 --> 00:07:22,580
most of his subjects because they wouldn't have spoken what he would recognise as French.

164
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:27,300
They would have spoken dialects of French or they'd have spoken Flemish or Occitan or Breton.

165
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:27,560
Yeah.

166
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:30,100
Or German, all of these kinds of things.

167
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:32,980
So Louis has been brought up to be king.

168
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:36,240
And he, we think of him as an absolute monarch.

169
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:42,340
And he had copied out as a teenager a text to tell him that French kings had received from God himself

170
00:07:42,340 --> 00:07:47,380
the greatest and most absolute power over other men that has ever been entrusted to one man.

171
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:51,580
But that absolute power is very tightly circumscribed.

172
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:54,180
So first of all, he relies on lots of other people.

173
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:57,220
He relies on judges and nobles and officials.

174
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:02,820
He's got his entendants, they're called, the royal officials that they send out to the kingdom.

175
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:05,700
If he loses their support, that's very bad for him.

176
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:06,400
He needs them.

177
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:08,700
Because he does have a famous exchange, doesn't he?

178
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:10,160
Is it 1787?

179
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:14,960
Where he basically, someone in the Parliament was saying this is illegal.

180
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:18,080
And Louis famously says it's legal because I say it is.

181
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:20,880
But that's a mark of weakness, isn't it?

182
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:22,680
Rather than strength that he has to say that.

183
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:23,220
Right.

184
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:27,120
Because France does have other institutions and constitutional conventions.

185
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:31,120
And they are so complicated that nobody really understands them.

186
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:40,460
So his grandfather, Louis XV, in the 1750s, had said, you know, France is such a kind of multi-layered, complicated machine.

187
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:45,240
I'd like to get all this written down on paper so I can make sense of it.

188
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:49,000
And he'd commissioned constitutional scholars to make a compilation of documents.

189
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:52,360
And they came back to him eventually and they just said, we're going to give up.

190
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:52,900
It's not possible.

191
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:56,400
It's much too complicated because it's a bit like Britain.

192
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:57,100
It is.

193
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:00,560
It is a massive patchwork of conventions and customs and laws.

194
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:03,560
But Dominic, you said you compared it to a machine.

195
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,140
And we talked about how Louis XVI is a great one for tinkering in a workshop.

196
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:11,900
Do you think that this is a frustration to him?

197
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:20,800
That he, as someone who obviously likes contraptions and working out how things function, that the state kind of defies his ability to do that?

198
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:21,940
I think absolutely.

199
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:26,740
Because I think Louis, like other people of his generation, is a product.

200
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:28,300
He's a product of the mid-18th century.

201
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:31,400
You know, he comes from a different world from Louis XIV.

202
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:33,700
He's an Enlightenment man to some degree.

203
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:39,920
And that doesn't mean he's sitting there reading Voltaire, but it means he's in that, you know, he's in that context.

204
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:41,040
Things can be solved.

205
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:41,960
Things can be sorted.

206
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:48,420
He's also been brought up, by the way, to respect what he sees as the rights of his citizens.

207
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:51,260
I know you're going to be talking a lot about rights in a subsequent episode.

208
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:57,100
And Louis had written down in his kind of exercise book as a boy that the rights of his citizens were light.

209
00:09:57,100 --> 00:10:00,120
They were life, honour, liberty and property.

210
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:05,760
Not so dissimilar from the language of the American tax dodgers, Tom.

211
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:11,900
So his regime, the Ancien Régime, as people say, the old regime.

212
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:19,080
In your sort of Charles Dickens, Taylor Two Cities version of the French Revolution, it's corrupt and cruel, isn't it?

213
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:20,740
There's Marquesses kind of…

214
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:22,660
Running over paupers in their carriages.

215
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:24,180
Exactly. All of that stuff.

216
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:26,680
And very antiquated and reactionary.

217
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:32,960
In reality, that regime is very, very modern and is changing all the time.

218
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:36,720
Nobody says we are living in the Ancien Régime.

219
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:37,980
We're living in the old regime.

220
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:43,440
They think they are living in an age of enormous change and modernity.

221
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:45,940
And we mentioned already Simon Schama.

222
00:10:46,080 --> 00:10:49,040
In his book Citizens, he's brilliant at bringing all this out.

223
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:55,060
And he says, you know, wherever you look as a historian, there are signs of great excitement and dynamism and energy.

224
00:10:55,380 --> 00:10:56,880
So he mentions, you know.

225
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:58,440
French trade, French industry.

226
00:10:58,780 --> 00:11:01,440
It's the home of chemistry, of maths.

227
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:04,160
Lavoisier, who will end up losing his head, won't he?

228
00:11:04,580 --> 00:11:10,440
Both Schama and Robert Danton, in their books about this, emphasize hot air balloons.

229
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:13,760
Ballooning is a great spectacle and a great…

230
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:17,160
You know, French ballooning is the AI of the day.

231
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:19,080
It's something that people are talking about.

232
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:21,680
They don't know what world it will bring.

233
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:23,000
It's tremendously exciting.

234
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:26,220
The first flight was from the Bois de Boulogne.

235
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:27,540
In 1783.

236
00:11:27,860 --> 00:11:31,460
And people would say, Robert Danton quotes some periodicals.

237
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:33,340
We live in the century of inventions.

238
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:35,040
We're surrounded by miracles.

239
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:38,800
Genius is everywhere, conquering nature and the elements.

240
00:11:39,140 --> 00:11:41,020
And that's very Enlightenment, isn't it?

241
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:41,840
It's totally Enlightenment.

242
00:11:41,840 --> 00:11:45,940
So Benjamin Franklin is a great hero in Paris.

243
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:48,320
And he's a great one for these kind of experiments.

244
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:49,460
Absolutely, yeah.

245
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:52,540
Because, of course, the whole American republic is an experiment, isn't it?

246
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:54,920
So America comes to be identified.

247
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:58,340
Both with nature and the kind of beauties and simplicity of nature,

248
00:11:58,540 --> 00:12:02,740
but also with this idea of kind of progress, of modernity.

249
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:07,040
To what extent is the existence of the American republic

250
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:12,200
a kind of standing encouragement to people in France to think,

251
00:12:12,260 --> 00:12:14,580
yeah, we could rebuild in that way?

252
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,020
It's a good question.

253
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:18,700
I think people are fascinated by the American experiment.

254
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:22,200
They're curious to see how it works out.

255
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:25,680
People like Benjamin Franklin are very popular in France.

256
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:30,080
But that said, if America didn't exist,

257
00:12:30,340 --> 00:12:31,660
the French Revolution would still have happened.

258
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:32,500
Would still have happened, sure.

259
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:35,900
So I don't think it's a direct inspiration,

260
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,140
but I think some of the language definitely,

261
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:42,900
there's a commonality of language between the French and American revolution.

262
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:44,140
And just going on from that,

263
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:46,980
the crown support for the American revolution

264
00:12:46,980 --> 00:12:51,620
obviously will play a key part in its ultimate bankruptcy.

265
00:12:51,620 --> 00:12:57,740
But is there also a sense that the fact that a monarch

266
00:12:57,740 --> 00:13:02,060
is going to the assistance of rebels against another monarch

267
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:06,000
an example of someone sitting on a tree, sitting on a branch

268
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:07,740
and sawing off the branch?

269
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:09,400
Yeah, I think it probably is.

270
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:10,800
I mean, there are people in the French regime

271
00:13:10,800 --> 00:13:12,620
who are very nervous about the idea of doing this.

272
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:14,320
For that reason?

273
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:16,920
Well, they're more worried about the finances.

274
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:18,920
But I just wondered whether there was a kind of, you know,

275
00:13:18,940 --> 00:13:20,760
an ideological anxiety about it as well.

276
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:21,440
Yeah.

277
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:25,020
Because Louis XVI seems not particularly to have been worried about that.

278
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:26,800
I don't think they're thinking about the American Republic

279
00:13:28,260 --> 00:13:30,340
so closely as that, to be honest with you.

280
00:13:30,420 --> 00:13:33,140
I think they're more interested in giving Britain a bloody nose.

281
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,800
Anyway, so all this excitement about change means you have two things.

282
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:39,420
You have people, we have a trend of sense of possibility on the one hand.

283
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:40,340
We can do anything.

284
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:42,340
We can conquer nature, so what can't we do?

285
00:13:42,620 --> 00:13:43,700
And then the other hand, of course,

286
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:45,240
there are always people who lose out from change.

287
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:47,480
So in this, just to give you a small example,

288
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:50,180
if you're a weaver or an artisan or something,

289
00:13:50,180 --> 00:13:53,940
there are new businesses, new industrialized businesses coming up

290
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:54,940
that may put you out of work.

291
00:13:55,040 --> 00:13:57,360
And so you would like to blame somebody.

292
00:13:57,560 --> 00:13:58,920
And we'll come to that later on.

293
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:01,440
As part of that modernity,

294
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:04,420
I think you also get a new political culture, especially in Paris.

295
00:14:05,940 --> 00:14:08,360
This is not created by the French Revolution.

296
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:10,500
It creates the revolution.

297
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:11,360
Right.

298
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:14,980
Because we were talking about that in the context of the kind of

299
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:16,900
pornographic abuse of Marie Antoinette.

300
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:17,480
Yeah, the pamphlets.

301
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:18,220
Exactly.

302
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:20,660
Robert Danton's book, The Revolutionary Temper,

303
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:22,240
is absolutely wonderful on all this,

304
00:14:22,280 --> 00:14:25,060
on the coffeehouses, on people swapping ideas.

305
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:28,240
People would read pamphlets out on street corners.

306
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:30,880
There's a new term, which we're very familiar with,

307
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:33,900
created in 1750 to describe this,

308
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:36,280
opinion publique, public opinion.

309
00:14:36,720 --> 00:14:38,500
That phrase was not used before

310
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:40,520
because it didn't really exist in the same way.

311
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:41,840
So there's that.

312
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:44,860
But also I think there is also a really important thing,

313
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:48,140
which is a new language that people use to discuss politics.

314
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:52,460
So we were saying that Louis, you talked about him and the locks,

315
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:57,240
the idea of tinkering and the world being a machine and improving it.

316
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:59,700
And that's very much a product of the age of reason.

317
00:15:00,700 --> 00:15:04,560
But actually, when he becomes king and he's 19 years old,

318
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:06,180
there is a generation emerging,

319
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:10,220
this kind of generation below him who are much more romantic.

320
00:15:10,860 --> 00:15:14,380
They are the people who are loving Rousseau, Jean-Jacques Rousseau.

321
00:15:14,440 --> 00:15:16,320
Well, but not entirely because Marie Antoinette,

322
00:15:16,380 --> 00:15:17,120
who is his generation.

323
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:18,320
I mean, she's part of that.

324
00:15:18,420 --> 00:15:20,460
I mean, she is the great embodiment of that in a way.

325
00:15:20,660 --> 00:15:23,980
It's definitely younger people who embrace that.

326
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:25,500
But of course, it percolates out.

327
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:28,540
So Marie Antoinette is absolutely a classic example

328
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:31,960
of somebody who absorbs this cult of sensibility,

329
00:15:32,160 --> 00:15:34,980
you know, nature, simplicity, all of these things,

330
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:37,620
intense feeling, tenderness, friendship, family.

331
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:43,060
And also the idea that if you obey the impulses of your emotions,

332
00:15:43,580 --> 00:15:45,440
that will lead you to truth.

333
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:45,940
Yeah.

334
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:46,900
So it's kind of bizarre.

335
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:47,340
Isn't it?

336
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:49,060
That simultaneously, it's an age of reason,

337
00:15:49,640 --> 00:15:51,640
but it's also an age of intense feeling.

338
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:53,820
And the assumption seems to be that the two,

339
00:15:53,940 --> 00:15:55,520
well, there is a tension there,

340
00:15:55,580 --> 00:15:57,860
but there seems to be an assumption that there isn't.

341
00:15:58,020 --> 00:15:59,900
Well, I think there was a slight generational issue.

342
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,940
So I think if you were born in 1740,

343
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:06,760
let's say, you grow up in an age that is polite,

344
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,080
witty, ironic, sceptical.

345
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,040
Voltaire, you know, Voltaire is very sceptical.

346
00:16:12,460 --> 00:16:13,660
They love poking fun.

347
00:16:14,540 --> 00:16:16,560
There's always a kind of wry smile

348
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:18,940
at the corner of your mouth, that kind of attitude.

349
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:20,300
They're basically quite British.

350
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:24,240
And I think if you are a generation later,

351
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:25,740
let's say you're born in 1760,

352
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,680
then that scepticism and stuff,

353
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:31,500
you find that a bit desiccated maybe.

354
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:32,300
It leaves you cold.

355
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:34,660
What you're all about is your heart,

356
00:16:34,760 --> 00:16:36,900
is living your truth, Tom.

357
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:37,580
Living your truth.

358
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:38,640
Yes, absolutely.

359
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:42,860
You know, tears, crying is really important to these people

360
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:44,260
and is important in the revolution.

361
00:16:44,260 --> 00:16:47,500
Tears are seen as a sign of authenticity and honesty.

362
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:49,920
So people are always breaking down in tears

363
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:51,500
in the revolutionary story.

364
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:54,560
And the idea that underpins all of this

365
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:56,020
is the idea of virtue.

366
00:16:56,360 --> 00:16:58,460
And I was thinking when I was writing the notes

367
00:16:59,620 --> 00:17:01,500
how virtue in the French Revolution

368
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:03,260
is the diversity of its day.

369
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:05,540
In other words, it is an abstract noun

370
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:10,280
that if you have any decency as a person at all,

371
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:11,200
you're in favour of it.

372
00:17:11,260 --> 00:17:13,760
And if you start to poke fun at it or to say,

373
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:15,640
well, I just think it's all overblown.

374
00:17:15,700 --> 00:17:16,600
It's the emperor's new clothes.

375
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:17,500
I don't really agree with it.

376
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:20,500
People say, you're not just old, you're evil.

377
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:21,780
You say virtue.

378
00:17:22,460 --> 00:17:25,220
I mean, you might more properly say virtus,

379
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:28,140
which is the Latin word from which the French word comes

380
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:32,420
and actually has the sense of manliness as well.

381
00:17:32,660 --> 00:17:34,860
And I think one of the dimensions of the French Revolution,

382
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:37,180
which we will be exploring over the course of this series

383
00:17:37,180 --> 00:17:38,400
and subsequent series on it,

384
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:43,120
is the way in which actually the kind of the egalite bros

385
00:17:43,760 --> 00:17:45,500
are quite sexist.

386
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:46,480
Yeah, of course.

387
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:49,360
It is a very, very chauvinist movement,

388
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:50,700
I think, the French Revolution.

389
00:17:51,160 --> 00:17:51,880
I think that's right.

390
00:17:52,040 --> 00:17:54,260
So the man who embodies all this more than anybody else,

391
00:17:54,280 --> 00:17:56,440
by the way, I think is Maximilien Robespierre,

392
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:57,880
and we'll come to him later on.

393
00:17:57,940 --> 00:17:59,840
He absolutely drinks deeply of all this stuff.

394
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:03,900
So it's a very politicised world and very passionate,

395
00:18:04,040 --> 00:18:06,620
but it is also, I think it's worth emphasising,

396
00:18:06,640 --> 00:18:09,360
even before the Revolution, a violent world.

397
00:18:10,200 --> 00:18:12,480
In Citizens, Simon Sharma famously wrote

398
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:15,420
that violence was not just a by-product of politics,

399
00:18:15,540 --> 00:18:17,760
but violence was the Revolution itself.

400
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:18,720
And I think he's right,

401
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:20,980
but I think it's also worth emphasising

402
00:18:20,980 --> 00:18:23,160
that France was already quite violent.

403
00:18:23,380 --> 00:18:26,300
So any history of 18th century France

404
00:18:26,300 --> 00:18:30,160
is punctuated by riots, lynchings, looting,

405
00:18:31,060 --> 00:18:33,220
people being publicly beheaded,

406
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:36,220
prisoners being broken on the wheel,

407
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:39,440
troops firing into crowds.

408
00:18:39,780 --> 00:18:41,820
So in other words, when the Revolution starts,

409
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:44,200
and it doesn't start with one thing,

410
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:45,840
it starts with a succession of things,

411
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:49,000
a lot of these things have already happened before.

412
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:50,240
They're not novelties.

413
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:53,080
The Revolution is not a storm breaking

414
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:56,200
from an otherwise placid, beautiful blue sky.

415
00:18:56,920 --> 00:18:59,800
It's one storm cloud among many, Tom.

416
00:19:00,140 --> 00:19:02,280
We talked about tax and finance.

417
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:06,420
So money is at the absolute centre of it,

418
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:08,080
and we should get into some of the money.

419
00:19:08,220 --> 00:19:08,840
Follow the money.

420
00:19:09,100 --> 00:19:10,140
Follow the money, exactly.

421
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:11,040
We should follow the money.

422
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:13,300
So all early modern monarchies

423
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:15,600
suffered massive money problems.

424
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:17,560
I mean, it's the story of the English Civil War.

425
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:18,700
It's the story of the American Revolution.

426
00:19:19,660 --> 00:19:22,280
Monarchies trying to raise more funds.

427
00:19:22,460 --> 00:19:23,580
And the reason they're doing that

428
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:25,480
is because they're living in a new age

429
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:26,920
with antiquated systems.

430
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:28,500
There's been a military revolution.

431
00:19:28,960 --> 00:19:31,480
So they are funding bigger navies

432
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:33,000
and bigger armies than ever before.

433
00:19:33,180 --> 00:19:34,840
But also they're doing it on a global scale,

434
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:37,180
which they weren't doing 200 years earlier.

435
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:38,540
You know, Britain, France,

436
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:41,020
they have to maintain fleets all over the planet.

437
00:19:41,020 --> 00:19:42,280
And for France,

438
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:44,840
the fact that Britain has emerged

439
00:19:45,400 --> 00:19:47,540
as a rival power

440
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:49,500
and a larger economy,

441
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:51,980
I mean, it's unsettling

442
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:53,780
because France has not had to face that before.

443
00:19:53,960 --> 00:19:55,680
France has always been the largest power,

444
00:19:56,200 --> 00:19:57,080
economically speaking.

445
00:19:57,400 --> 00:19:59,860
And so that imposes massive strains, doesn't it?

446
00:19:59,940 --> 00:20:01,280
When you're talking about the navy,

447
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:03,780
the Royal Navy is the key to British power.

448
00:20:03,900 --> 00:20:06,040
And so the French have to have a navy.

449
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:09,340
But as you say, I mean, it's just unbelievable strain.

450
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:10,100
It is.

451
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:13,300
So France has been running up massive debts since the 1720s

452
00:20:13,940 --> 00:20:15,060
and doesn't have a national bank.

453
00:20:15,160 --> 00:20:16,200
Britain has a national bank,

454
00:20:16,260 --> 00:20:18,000
but France, for various reasons, doesn't.

455
00:20:19,460 --> 00:20:22,140
And France had had a kind of traumatic experience

456
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:25,420
with a slightly dodgy Scottish financier.

457
00:20:25,540 --> 00:20:25,860
John Law.

458
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:27,980
It's a kind of tulip mania bust.

459
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:30,080
There'd been a massive stock market bust

460
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:32,160
and that basically turned them off finance.

461
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:35,140
So they distrust the idea of a national bank.

462
00:20:35,300 --> 00:20:37,140
You know, that is when the word millionaire is coined.

463
00:20:37,620 --> 00:20:38,000
Is that so?

464
00:20:38,180 --> 00:20:38,620
Yeah.

465
00:20:38,620 --> 00:20:40,480
Because so many people become so rich

466
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:41,400
and then they lose it all.

467
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:44,080
Tom, when Theo gives us an absolute roasting

468
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:45,320
for going over time,

469
00:20:45,480 --> 00:20:47,080
it will definitely not be my fault.

470
00:20:47,480 --> 00:20:47,960
Sorry.

471
00:20:48,860 --> 00:20:50,800
So even before the American War,

472
00:20:51,260 --> 00:20:53,560
France has already got very, very big debts.

473
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:57,280
It's got 2 billion livres in debt

474
00:20:57,280 --> 00:21:01,200
and it's basically spending 60% of its budget

475
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:02,260
servicing that debt.

476
00:21:02,340 --> 00:21:03,200
So on interest payments.

477
00:21:03,440 --> 00:21:04,880
And this is growing all the time.

478
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:08,140
The interesting thing is that Britain's debt is much greater.

479
00:21:09,740 --> 00:21:11,460
Britain's debt is three times greater.

480
00:21:11,560 --> 00:21:13,360
But Britain can always raise new taxes

481
00:21:13,360 --> 00:21:14,580
through the House of Commons.

482
00:21:14,740 --> 00:21:15,900
The government needs no more money.

483
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,480
The House of Commons votes for it.

484
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:18,240
Bang, job done.

485
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:19,920
France can't do that.

486
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:22,240
Not only does it not have a national bank,

487
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:25,240
it doesn't have a regular elected assembly.

488
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:28,220
People do remember that there was once a thing

489
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:29,440
called the Estates General.

490
00:21:30,260 --> 00:21:32,740
It was last met in 1614, 1615.

491
00:21:32,900 --> 00:21:35,180
And nobody can really remember what it was,

492
00:21:35,380 --> 00:21:36,660
how it was organized,

493
00:21:36,660 --> 00:21:39,700
who voted for it or anything like that.

494
00:21:39,940 --> 00:21:41,800
It's the existence of the British Parliament

495
00:21:41,800 --> 00:21:43,380
that is able to raise money.

496
00:21:43,540 --> 00:21:45,860
Is this something that in the build up

497
00:21:45,860 --> 00:21:46,460
to the French Revolution,

498
00:21:46,740 --> 00:21:49,140
people are looking at enviously and saying,

499
00:21:49,180 --> 00:21:50,960
why don't we have a system like this?

500
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:51,280
Yes.

501
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:53,340
A lot of the revolutionaries at the beginning

502
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:55,740
basically want to turn France into Britain.

503
00:21:56,040 --> 00:21:58,440
They want a more liberal economy

504
00:21:58,440 --> 00:22:00,880
and they want a representative institution

505
00:22:01,220 --> 00:22:02,640
or vote tax increases.

506
00:22:02,800 --> 00:22:03,460
And they think, bang,

507
00:22:03,540 --> 00:22:05,040
that'll magically fix all our problems.

508
00:22:05,160 --> 00:22:06,200
So that's the Estates General.

509
00:22:06,340 --> 00:22:06,640
That's the Estates General.

510
00:22:06,660 --> 00:22:07,880
That's the only kind of precedent

511
00:22:07,880 --> 00:22:08,860
in French history they have.

512
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:11,460
So France is raising tax

513
00:22:11,460 --> 00:22:14,260
through a lot of very strange, antiquated things.

514
00:22:14,400 --> 00:22:15,600
They've got a property tax

515
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:16,880
that a lot of towns don't pay.

516
00:22:17,120 --> 00:22:20,160
They've got a very lucrative tax on salt.

517
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:22,180
But again, that just increases,

518
00:22:22,340 --> 00:22:23,660
that means there's loads of smuggling.

519
00:22:23,840 --> 00:22:25,900
And they have internal customs duties.

520
00:22:26,640 --> 00:22:29,540
You are a miller in, you know,

521
00:22:29,620 --> 00:22:30,780
Provence or something.

522
00:22:30,980 --> 00:22:32,680
You want to sell your grain in Paris.

523
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:34,820
You have to pay multiple duties

524
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:37,440
to get your grain from A to B.

525
00:22:37,700 --> 00:22:39,140
And that obviously is very bad

526
00:22:39,140 --> 00:22:40,200
for the internal economy

527
00:22:40,200 --> 00:22:41,440
if people are having to pay tax,

528
00:22:41,560 --> 00:22:43,880
basically when they step outside their own town

529
00:22:43,880 --> 00:22:45,060
to sell things.

530
00:22:45,300 --> 00:22:47,660
And the kind of thing that a workman

531
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:52,160
in his workshop like Louis XVI

532
00:22:52,160 --> 00:22:53,560
would want to fix.

533
00:22:54,080 --> 00:22:54,560
Exactly.

534
00:22:55,620 --> 00:22:56,880
So here's the thing.

535
00:22:57,320 --> 00:22:58,560
Why don't they fix it?

536
00:22:59,300 --> 00:23:01,680
Louis, when he comes in at the age of 19,

537
00:23:01,960 --> 00:23:04,100
he's tempted by the idea of fixing it.

538
00:23:04,100 --> 00:23:07,700
But the issue is that whenever he comes up

539
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:08,940
against opposition,

540
00:23:09,340 --> 00:23:10,980
he almost always backs down.

541
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:13,740
So right at the beginning of his reign,

542
00:23:13,940 --> 00:23:15,260
he had a bloke called Turgot

543
00:23:15,260 --> 00:23:17,520
who was a liberal reformer.

544
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:19,120
He was basically slightly Liz Truss.

545
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:21,320
He wanted to go for growth,

546
00:23:22,600 --> 00:23:23,600
deregulate the economy,

547
00:23:23,820 --> 00:23:24,980
all of this kind of stuff.

548
00:23:26,160 --> 00:23:27,800
He liberalized bread prices.

549
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:29,460
Of course, that meant the bread prices went up.

550
00:23:29,500 --> 00:23:30,280
There was lots of looting.

551
00:23:30,320 --> 00:23:33,260
And Louis eventually basically got rid of him,

552
00:23:33,260 --> 00:23:35,500
said, you're pushing too far, too fast.

553
00:23:35,740 --> 00:23:37,820
So Dominic, your case might be

554
00:23:37,820 --> 00:23:39,340
that the problem with Louis isn't

555
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:40,640
that he is too despotic,

556
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:42,060
but that he's not despotic enough.

557
00:23:42,400 --> 00:23:44,180
If he'd been a charismatic

558
00:23:44,180 --> 00:23:46,560
and sufficiently adept statesman,

559
00:23:47,080 --> 00:23:49,680
that he could take a bit of unpopularity

560
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:51,520
and he could persuade the people he needed

561
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:54,160
and he could show concrete results,

562
00:23:54,980 --> 00:23:56,540
and he'd stuck to his guns,

563
00:23:56,760 --> 00:23:58,200
he might have been a much,

564
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:00,400
much more effective King of France.

565
00:24:00,480 --> 00:24:01,800
But as it is, what he will do is

566
00:24:01,800 --> 00:24:03,040
he will always gesture in the direction

567
00:24:03,040 --> 00:24:04,840
of change and then back down

568
00:24:04,840 --> 00:24:06,300
when he gets into trouble.

569
00:24:06,660 --> 00:24:08,880
So as Sharma says, and I quote,

570
00:24:09,060 --> 00:24:10,320
he wanted to be loved.

571
00:24:10,500 --> 00:24:12,900
And that is not an ideal thing for a King

572
00:24:12,900 --> 00:24:15,060
in a time of tremendous change and challenge.

573
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:17,360
So in the next few years,

574
00:24:17,380 --> 00:24:18,760
the problem actually gets a lot worse.

575
00:24:18,880 --> 00:24:20,480
And it gets worse, as you said,

576
00:24:20,600 --> 00:24:22,720
because they joined the American war.

577
00:24:23,120 --> 00:24:25,020
So Turgot's eventual successor,

578
00:24:25,140 --> 00:24:27,860
a guy called, a Swiss banker called Necker,

579
00:24:28,100 --> 00:24:28,940
Jean-Jacques Necker.

580
00:24:29,300 --> 00:24:30,020
Great name.

581
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:33,020
He says, you know, go for that American war.

582
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:35,940
Let's help the rebels in the American colonies.

583
00:24:36,180 --> 00:24:39,320
And he organizes lots of more short-term loans

584
00:24:39,320 --> 00:24:40,180
to pay for it.

585
00:24:40,480 --> 00:24:42,140
Now, it's a naval war,

586
00:24:42,320 --> 00:24:44,880
but as we know from the rest of his history,

587
00:24:44,940 --> 00:24:46,200
nothing is more expensive

588
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:48,780
than an 18th century top-notch navy.

589
00:24:49,600 --> 00:24:51,420
So they run up huge debts,

590
00:24:51,500 --> 00:24:52,680
one and a half billion livres

591
00:24:52,680 --> 00:24:54,860
on top of their existing debts,

592
00:24:55,500 --> 00:24:57,280
90% of which comes from loans.

593
00:24:57,460 --> 00:24:59,120
This will be a brilliant gamble,

594
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:01,440
by the way, if you get the Caribbean.

595
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:03,020
Because the Caribbean is so rich.

596
00:25:03,040 --> 00:25:03,760
Yeah.

597
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:04,860
If you get that,

598
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:06,480
that's why they really have joined the war.

599
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:10,080
They want to get Jamaica, places like that.

600
00:25:10,220 --> 00:25:12,560
But that requires them to inflict a defeat on Britain

601
00:25:12,560 --> 00:25:15,860
more total than the ultimate defeat is.

602
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:19,500
So although the British lose those 13 North American colonies,

603
00:25:20,020 --> 00:25:22,120
they keep lots of the rest of North America.

604
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:23,520
They don't lose any of the Caribbean,

605
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:24,680
which was their main priority.

606
00:25:25,040 --> 00:25:25,880
And so actually,

607
00:25:25,920 --> 00:25:27,980
the French have basically run up huge debts

608
00:25:27,980 --> 00:25:29,340
and they've got nothing to show for it.

609
00:25:29,860 --> 00:25:31,180
And Simon Sharma says,

610
00:25:31,380 --> 00:25:32,920
without much exaggeration,

611
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:34,700
it can be said that it was the cost of the war

612
00:25:34,700 --> 00:25:38,160
that brought on the terminal crisis of the French monarchy.

613
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:40,780
Should we get to the terminal crisis?

614
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:41,320
Yes.

615
00:25:41,380 --> 00:25:42,900
So let's fast forward three years

616
00:25:42,900 --> 00:25:45,860
to the year of the Diamond Necklace Trial.

617
00:25:45,940 --> 00:25:47,480
So now we're in 1786.

618
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:49,960
The finance minister now

619
00:25:49,960 --> 00:25:52,720
is a guy called Charles Alexandre de Calonne.

620
00:25:53,300 --> 00:25:54,380
He's very rich.

621
00:25:54,460 --> 00:25:55,160
He's very urbane.

622
00:25:55,300 --> 00:25:57,380
He's very sort of witty.

623
00:25:57,940 --> 00:25:59,320
He's a stock market player

624
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:00,820
and therefore everybody hates him.

625
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:01,300
You know,

626
00:26:01,300 --> 00:26:03,600
for understandable reasons.

627
00:26:03,880 --> 00:26:04,400
Bankers.

628
00:26:04,520 --> 00:26:06,260
And in August 1786,

629
00:26:06,680 --> 00:26:08,780
Calonne goes to see Louis XVI

630
00:26:08,780 --> 00:26:09,420
and he says,

631
00:26:10,140 --> 00:26:12,760
I've got a bit of a bombshell for you.

632
00:26:13,980 --> 00:26:16,920
We are looking into the financial abyss.

633
00:26:17,300 --> 00:26:18,200
He says,

634
00:26:18,240 --> 00:26:21,460
our annual deficit every year is 80 million livres

635
00:26:21,460 --> 00:26:23,800
and half of all our revenue

636
00:26:23,800 --> 00:26:25,900
is now being spent on paying the interest

637
00:26:25,900 --> 00:26:27,200
on our existing loans.

638
00:26:27,540 --> 00:26:28,360
And he says,

639
00:26:28,460 --> 00:26:29,800
what is worse,

640
00:26:30,960 --> 00:26:32,500
NECA's short-term loans

641
00:26:32,500 --> 00:26:34,860
that he took out to pay for the war in America

642
00:26:35,480 --> 00:26:37,060
are about to fall due

643
00:26:37,060 --> 00:26:40,420
and we don't have any money to pay them back.

644
00:26:41,720 --> 00:26:42,920
And he says to Louis,

645
00:26:43,080 --> 00:26:45,540
we have to totally reform the constitution.

646
00:26:45,660 --> 00:26:47,640
We just have to sort out this problem

647
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:49,740
with the tax system once and for all

648
00:26:49,740 --> 00:26:50,920
and get new taxes

649
00:26:50,920 --> 00:26:52,900
because we can't rely on new loans

650
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:53,840
to pay the old loans.

651
00:26:54,540 --> 00:26:55,440
So you may say,

652
00:26:55,520 --> 00:26:56,640
well, what is this problem?

653
00:26:56,740 --> 00:26:58,400
Why don't they just bring in new taxes?

654
00:26:58,580 --> 00:26:59,780
And the reason is,

655
00:26:59,780 --> 00:27:02,940
France is a society based on the idea

656
00:27:02,940 --> 00:27:04,900
of hierarchy and privileges.

657
00:27:05,300 --> 00:27:08,780
So if you are a nobleman or a clergyman,

658
00:27:09,400 --> 00:27:10,800
you don't pay tax.

659
00:27:11,060 --> 00:27:12,840
And people don't think of that

660
00:27:12,840 --> 00:27:15,340
as a disgraceful exemption.

661
00:27:15,760 --> 00:27:19,160
They think that's the essence of our liberties.

662
00:27:19,540 --> 00:27:21,280
These are our God-given

663
00:27:21,280 --> 00:27:25,880
and our ancestral rights as Frenchmen.

664
00:27:25,980 --> 00:27:27,920
And it's a source of aspiration, isn't it,

665
00:27:27,980 --> 00:27:29,020
for people?

666
00:27:29,020 --> 00:27:32,180
Because it is possible to enter the clergy

667
00:27:32,180 --> 00:27:35,680
or to gain patents of nobility.

668
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:38,000
So it's a kind of incentive

669
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:39,800
to stick with the system.

670
00:27:39,940 --> 00:27:40,440
It is.

671
00:27:40,500 --> 00:27:41,820
And also people don't like the idea

672
00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:44,120
of a uniform system like a machine.

673
00:27:44,300 --> 00:27:46,320
They say that is what will destroy liberty.

674
00:27:46,680 --> 00:27:49,220
Liberty lies in difference and in quirks

675
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:50,800
and eccentricities and traditions.

676
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:53,700
And a government that tries to erase that

677
00:27:53,700 --> 00:27:55,840
and just to put the stamp of royal authority

678
00:27:55,840 --> 00:27:57,280
and say everybody must pay

679
00:27:57,280 --> 00:27:59,380
is a despotic government.

680
00:27:59,640 --> 00:28:00,760
And we don't want that.

681
00:28:00,760 --> 00:28:02,580
We want people to stand up for our traditions.

682
00:28:03,400 --> 00:28:05,120
And the people they want in particular

683
00:28:05,120 --> 00:28:07,540
are these institutions called the parlement.

684
00:28:07,840 --> 00:28:10,080
Now, confusingly, they're not parliaments.

685
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:10,680
Right.

686
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:14,160
So these are the ones that the parlement of Paris

687
00:28:14,160 --> 00:28:15,540
that the Cardinal de Roma

688
00:28:16,150 --> 00:28:18,120
opted to be tried by rather than the king.

689
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:19,020
They're courts.

690
00:28:19,020 --> 00:28:20,140
They're high courts, basically.

691
00:28:20,740 --> 00:28:23,240
And around them, so the parlement in Paris,

692
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:25,760
around them it employs hundreds of people,

693
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:26,780
clerks and stuff.

694
00:28:26,780 --> 00:28:29,020
It's got its own kind of quarter of the city.

695
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:31,760
There's a whole kind of community of lawyers

696
00:28:31,760 --> 00:28:33,380
and notaries around it.

697
00:28:33,500 --> 00:28:35,460
And people invest it with tremendous,

698
00:28:35,660 --> 00:28:37,580
you know, it's culturally very meaningful.

699
00:28:37,740 --> 00:28:39,320
People invest it with a lot of significance.

700
00:28:40,140 --> 00:28:42,740
The parliaments, these high courts,

701
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:45,720
when the king introduces an edict,

702
00:28:45,840 --> 00:28:47,580
they have to register it.

703
00:28:47,620 --> 00:28:49,740
And they have to say, yep, fine.

704
00:28:50,080 --> 00:28:52,740
It doesn't contravene the rights of French people

705
00:28:52,740 --> 00:28:55,520
that you as a king have sworn to uphold.

706
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:58,760
And what's happened, there's a pattern

707
00:28:58,760 --> 00:29:01,620
that basically since the 1750s,

708
00:29:01,680 --> 00:29:05,120
Louis XV and Louis XVI have asked the parlement

709
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:07,880
to approve to register new taxes

710
00:29:07,880 --> 00:29:10,780
and the parlement has bitterly resisted.

711
00:29:11,160 --> 00:29:12,840
And they have said increasingly,

712
00:29:13,420 --> 00:29:15,300
the reason we're resisting the new taxes

713
00:29:15,300 --> 00:29:16,760
is because we're defending liberty

714
00:29:16,760 --> 00:29:18,380
against your despotism.

715
00:29:18,520 --> 00:29:20,040
We're defending the nation

716
00:29:20,040 --> 00:29:22,420
against your court in Versailles.

717
00:29:22,520 --> 00:29:25,500
We're defending the ancient French constitution

718
00:29:25,500 --> 00:29:30,360
against your suffocating, joyless absolutism.

719
00:29:30,520 --> 00:29:33,720
And does the physical separation

720
00:29:34,380 --> 00:29:36,000
of the king in Versailles

721
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,200
from the parlement, say, in Paris,

722
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:41,960
that presumably is enhancing that sense

723
00:29:41,960 --> 00:29:44,720
of separation between the monarchy

724
00:29:44,720 --> 00:29:47,140
and the sense the parlement have

725
00:29:47,140 --> 00:29:48,920
that they are defending the people's rights?

726
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:50,440
I think so, Tom. I think absolutely.

727
00:29:50,600 --> 00:29:51,940
I think it's a real problem actually

728
00:29:51,940 --> 00:29:54,260
for Louis XVI. Geography.

729
00:29:54,260 --> 00:29:56,860
The fact that Versailles is 12 miles from Paris.

730
00:29:57,040 --> 00:29:58,580
He never leaves Versailles,

731
00:29:58,740 --> 00:30:00,220
or barely ever leaves Versailles.

732
00:30:00,440 --> 00:30:03,760
That 12 miles, most ordinary people can't afford

733
00:30:03,760 --> 00:30:05,940
to take an expensive coach to Versailles.

734
00:30:06,160 --> 00:30:08,780
So he's always slightly sealed off.

735
00:30:09,200 --> 00:30:10,860
He's always in a bit of a bubble

736
00:30:10,860 --> 00:30:13,080
and there's a real resentment of that bubble.

737
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:14,740
It creates the idea of a corrupt,

738
00:30:14,920 --> 00:30:16,880
out of touch court.

739
00:30:17,560 --> 00:30:19,900
And you get the sense that Paris

740
00:30:19,900 --> 00:30:23,440
is this kind of place seething with political excitement

741
00:30:23,440 --> 00:30:26,960
and theatre and new ideas and newspapers.

742
00:30:27,660 --> 00:30:30,420
And Versailles is this sort of airless world

743
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:32,140
of snobbery and ritual.

744
00:30:32,440 --> 00:30:34,580
And I think it's a really good point.

745
00:30:34,620 --> 00:30:35,160
I agree with you.

746
00:30:35,160 --> 00:30:36,340
I think it's a huge problem for Louis.

747
00:30:37,020 --> 00:30:38,900
So anyway, Calonne says to Louis,

748
00:30:39,040 --> 00:30:40,200
look, we've got this huge problem.

749
00:30:40,740 --> 00:30:43,080
The parlement are never going to give us what we want.

750
00:30:43,220 --> 00:30:45,280
We have to bypass the parlement.

751
00:30:45,660 --> 00:30:47,240
We have to go over their heads.

752
00:30:47,640 --> 00:30:49,040
I'm going to call an assembly

753
00:30:49,040 --> 00:30:51,460
of the greatest men in the kingdom, the notables.

754
00:30:51,920 --> 00:30:53,420
If they agree with this,

755
00:30:53,440 --> 00:30:54,440
with the idea of the new taxes,

756
00:30:54,540 --> 00:30:57,000
then maybe we can basically just ignore the parlement

757
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:57,760
and do what we like.

758
00:30:57,900 --> 00:30:59,660
And this idea of an assembly of notables,

759
00:30:59,740 --> 00:31:02,160
this is, again, something with the ancient precedent

760
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:04,680
that is being taken out of the lumber box

761
00:31:04,680 --> 00:31:06,260
of antiquated traditions.

762
00:31:06,540 --> 00:31:08,100
Exactly. Yeah, it is. Exactly.

763
00:31:08,460 --> 00:31:09,920
And Louis says to Calonne,

764
00:31:11,260 --> 00:31:12,480
I'm not sure about this.

765
00:31:12,580 --> 00:31:13,980
I mean, this is a very big step

766
00:31:13,980 --> 00:31:16,060
to try to bypass the institutions

767
00:31:16,060 --> 00:31:17,180
to get this money.

768
00:31:17,500 --> 00:31:19,540
And Calonne says, trust me,

769
00:31:19,580 --> 00:31:22,200
if you do this, you will go down in history

770
00:31:22,200 --> 00:31:25,120
as the man whose courage and foresight

771
00:31:25,120 --> 00:31:27,520
solved the financial problems

772
00:31:27,520 --> 00:31:29,360
of the French monarchy forever.

773
00:31:29,680 --> 00:31:31,340
And the historian Jeremy Popkin

774
00:31:31,340 --> 00:31:34,780
has a very strong line after this in his book.

775
00:31:34,860 --> 00:31:37,700
He says, and so Louis XVI allowed Calonne

776
00:31:37,700 --> 00:31:40,240
to set in motion the series of events

777
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:43,040
that would lead to the complete overthrow

778
00:31:43,040 --> 00:31:44,180
of his monarchy.

779
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:47,220
This assembly of the notables

780
00:31:47,220 --> 00:31:48,440
has gathered.

781
00:31:48,780 --> 00:31:50,060
With what consequence?

782
00:31:50,060 --> 00:31:52,780
So basically, Calonne has brought together

783
00:31:52,780 --> 00:31:54,080
all the bigwigs in the kingdom,

784
00:31:54,200 --> 00:31:58,860
princes, archbishops, judges, dukes,

785
00:31:59,340 --> 00:32:00,340
all of these people.

786
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:02,800
And he says to them, OK, here's my plan.

787
00:32:03,780 --> 00:32:05,060
We're going to abolish all these

788
00:32:05,060 --> 00:32:05,980
mad tax privileges.

789
00:32:06,560 --> 00:32:07,880
We're just going to have one tax

790
00:32:07,880 --> 00:32:10,980
on landed property, and irrespective

791
00:32:10,980 --> 00:32:12,860
of your social status, you'll have to pay.

792
00:32:13,100 --> 00:32:15,480
So he is asking turkeys to vote for Christmas, basically.

793
00:32:15,640 --> 00:32:16,660
He is, but he's saying, look,

794
00:32:16,760 --> 00:32:17,760
this is in the interest of France.

795
00:32:17,860 --> 00:32:18,760
Come on, we've got to do this.

796
00:32:18,760 --> 00:32:19,960
We don't want to keep losing to the British.

797
00:32:20,220 --> 00:32:20,560
Sure.

798
00:32:20,740 --> 00:32:23,880
Number two, he says, we're going to lift

799
00:32:23,880 --> 00:32:25,620
some of these mad, antiquated regulations.

800
00:32:26,440 --> 00:32:28,840
Let's have the same, you pay the same duty

801
00:32:28,840 --> 00:32:30,300
wherever you are in France.

802
00:32:30,460 --> 00:32:32,480
Let's have freer markets so people

803
00:32:32,480 --> 00:32:33,720
can sell across France.

804
00:32:34,240 --> 00:32:36,980
Let's, you know, all of these vested interests,

805
00:32:37,900 --> 00:32:39,740
we can relax some of these rules.

806
00:32:39,880 --> 00:32:41,000
So we'll have more competition,

807
00:32:41,180 --> 00:32:41,860
we'll have more growth.

808
00:32:42,420 --> 00:32:44,620
And number three, he says, I know I'm asking you a lot,

809
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:47,900
so we're going to have elected local assemblies,

810
00:32:47,900 --> 00:32:50,040
regular elected local assemblies,

811
00:32:50,160 --> 00:32:52,200
and basically you, the landowners,

812
00:32:52,300 --> 00:32:53,560
the bigwigs will dominate them.

813
00:32:53,920 --> 00:32:56,300
So, you know, you'll get your way.

814
00:32:56,460 --> 00:32:58,480
Your privileges will remain intact.

815
00:32:59,340 --> 00:33:02,360
It's a very, very enlightened scheme.

816
00:33:03,160 --> 00:33:05,420
It's a very mid-18th century scheme.

817
00:33:05,800 --> 00:33:06,980
It's rational.

818
00:33:07,140 --> 00:33:07,860
It's sensible.

819
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:10,320
It's kind of, let's have a bit of uniformity

820
00:33:10,320 --> 00:33:11,760
and sort this thing out.

821
00:33:11,920 --> 00:33:13,320
So it's not going to work, in other words.

822
00:33:13,360 --> 00:33:13,500
Yeah.

823
00:33:13,520 --> 00:33:15,020
And the notables just hate it.

824
00:33:15,320 --> 00:33:16,920
They hate it because they don't like the,

825
00:33:16,920 --> 00:33:18,200
obviously they're paying the land tax.

826
00:33:18,600 --> 00:33:21,180
They don't like his plan for the assemblies

827
00:33:21,180 --> 00:33:22,500
because they say, he says,

828
00:33:22,640 --> 00:33:25,300
well, obviously you'd sit with the commons

829
00:33:25,300 --> 00:33:28,340
in your province together in one assembly.

830
00:33:28,580 --> 00:33:29,660
They don't like that at all.

831
00:33:29,720 --> 00:33:30,900
They say the commons,

832
00:33:30,900 --> 00:33:32,540
which they call the third estate,

833
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:34,220
they have to be separate.

834
00:33:34,380 --> 00:33:35,180
We're not sitting with those.

835
00:33:35,320 --> 00:33:35,760
Riff raff.

836
00:33:35,840 --> 00:33:36,480
Great unwashed.

837
00:33:36,520 --> 00:33:37,240
Yeah, riff raff.

838
00:33:37,940 --> 00:33:40,400
And they say it's always the game

839
00:33:40,400 --> 00:33:41,980
that these different institutions play.

840
00:33:42,120 --> 00:33:45,100
To basically kick the tax increases down the road,

841
00:33:45,140 --> 00:33:46,900
they say, you'd have to go through

842
00:33:46,920 --> 00:33:47,860
and call the estates general.

843
00:33:48,260 --> 00:33:49,920
I mean, no one really knows what that is,

844
00:33:50,040 --> 00:33:50,960
but they sort of,

845
00:33:50,980 --> 00:33:52,160
because they think it'll never happen,

846
00:33:52,200 --> 00:33:52,620
I suspect.

847
00:33:52,760 --> 00:33:55,260
They say, call the estates general

848
00:33:55,260 --> 00:33:55,980
to approve the taxes.

849
00:33:55,980 --> 00:33:57,280
It's like the Aztecs bringing out the owl.

850
00:33:58,100 --> 00:33:58,500
Right.

851
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:00,000
It's the ultimate recourse.

852
00:34:01,140 --> 00:34:02,820
You know, you need more representation.

853
00:34:03,280 --> 00:34:05,180
Basically, we don't want to carry the can

854
00:34:05,180 --> 00:34:06,360
for the tax increases.

855
00:34:07,100 --> 00:34:09,500
You have to get a bigger body to do it.

856
00:34:10,820 --> 00:34:12,760
Calon is really furious about this.

857
00:34:12,820 --> 00:34:13,760
And now he does something

858
00:34:14,200 --> 00:34:15,480
that is actually disastrous

859
00:34:15,480 --> 00:34:18,420
from the perspective of the French monarchy.

860
00:34:18,500 --> 00:34:19,420
He goes public.

861
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:21,620
He seeks to rouse the people.

862
00:34:21,820 --> 00:34:25,300
He sends a pamphlet out to every priest

863
00:34:25,300 --> 00:34:27,600
in the city in Paris.

864
00:34:27,900 --> 00:34:28,840
And he says,

865
00:34:28,940 --> 00:34:31,680
you have to read this to your congregation,

866
00:34:31,740 --> 00:34:32,920
your Sunday sermons.

867
00:34:33,080 --> 00:34:34,800
He has it reprinted in gazettes.

868
00:34:34,840 --> 00:34:36,440
He has it read out on street corners

869
00:34:36,440 --> 00:34:39,500
and read aloud to the crowds in Les Halles

870
00:34:39,500 --> 00:34:41,380
in the big markets.

871
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:45,200
And this thing, he says,

872
00:34:45,200 --> 00:34:46,240
he tells people,

873
00:34:46,400 --> 00:34:48,040
France is at a crossroads.

874
00:34:48,780 --> 00:34:51,260
You know, finances are in a total mess.

875
00:34:51,580 --> 00:34:53,320
Either we have total change

876
00:34:53,320 --> 00:34:54,780
or total collapse.

877
00:34:55,380 --> 00:34:56,840
And in this pamphlet,

878
00:34:56,940 --> 00:34:59,180
he uses a lot of the language

879
00:34:59,180 --> 00:35:01,400
that anticipates the revolution.

880
00:35:01,880 --> 00:35:04,400
So he's using terms like the national will,

881
00:35:05,160 --> 00:35:05,760
patriotism.

882
00:35:05,920 --> 00:35:06,780
He says,

883
00:35:06,920 --> 00:35:09,120
the privileged will be sacrificed.

884
00:35:09,400 --> 00:35:11,460
Yes, when justice requires it

885
00:35:11,460 --> 00:35:12,440
and need demands it.

886
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:14,040
Would it be better to tax again

887
00:35:14,040 --> 00:35:15,820
the unprivileged, the people?

888
00:35:16,360 --> 00:35:17,520
And that kind of language,

889
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:18,700
people haven't heard that

890
00:35:18,700 --> 00:35:23,080
from French royal finance ministers before.

891
00:35:23,320 --> 00:35:25,340
He's anticipating a lot of the terms

892
00:35:25,340 --> 00:35:27,100
that will be used in the revolution.

893
00:35:27,400 --> 00:35:29,440
Now, when he comes out with all this,

894
00:35:29,500 --> 00:35:31,640
the notables go absolutely ballistic.

895
00:35:32,140 --> 00:35:33,620
You know, how dare you

896
00:35:34,540 --> 00:35:36,320
rouse the people against us?

897
00:35:36,660 --> 00:35:38,340
And so they complain to Louis about him.

898
00:35:38,460 --> 00:35:39,400
So they complain to Louis.

899
00:35:39,460 --> 00:35:41,460
And Louis, as is always the way,

900
00:35:41,560 --> 00:35:42,700
Louis sacks him.

901
00:35:42,700 --> 00:35:43,900
Sorry, mate, you've got to go.

902
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:44,580
People don't like,

903
00:35:44,620 --> 00:35:45,600
the notables don't like it.

904
00:35:45,720 --> 00:35:46,740
The notables don't like it.

905
00:35:47,060 --> 00:35:48,740
As Sharma says in Citizens,

906
00:35:49,340 --> 00:35:51,100
by taking his case to the public,

907
00:35:51,240 --> 00:35:52,420
Calonne had made politics

908
00:35:52,420 --> 00:35:53,760
a matter of national attention.

909
00:35:54,560 --> 00:35:57,180
Once Pandora's box had been opened in this way,

910
00:35:57,360 --> 00:35:59,660
it proved impossible to close the lid.

911
00:35:59,860 --> 00:36:02,380
After Calonne, anything was possible.

912
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:03,860
We love a Pandora's box.

913
00:36:03,940 --> 00:36:06,000
I like the fact that Sharma's gone for Pandora's box.

914
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:07,420
So the Pandora's box has been opened

915
00:36:07,420 --> 00:36:09,080
and the storm clouds of revolution are gathering.

916
00:36:09,080 --> 00:36:09,640
Definitely they are.

917
00:36:10,320 --> 00:36:11,760
Now, the trouble is that,

918
00:36:11,760 --> 00:36:13,440
so the debate has started,

919
00:36:14,560 --> 00:36:15,680
a public debate.

920
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:17,360
People are talking about this in cafes,

921
00:36:17,460 --> 00:36:18,660
on street corners and things.

922
00:36:19,360 --> 00:36:22,340
And as amazing as it may sound to many listeners,

923
00:36:22,840 --> 00:36:24,820
most people take the notable side,

924
00:36:25,040 --> 00:36:26,880
the side of the very privileged people

925
00:36:26,880 --> 00:36:28,040
who don't want to pay more taxes.

926
00:36:28,320 --> 00:36:30,900
So Robert Darnton quotes journalists of the day.

927
00:36:31,100 --> 00:36:32,180
Our notables,

928
00:36:32,180 --> 00:36:34,140
which were thought to be puppets or courtiers,

929
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:36,920
are for the most part well-informed patriots

930
00:36:36,920 --> 00:36:39,040
who know the true interests of the nation.

931
00:36:39,720 --> 00:36:41,200
Because the one thing that people

932
00:36:41,200 --> 00:36:42,160
are really worried about

933
00:36:42,160 --> 00:36:44,980
is not the fact that nobles don't pay taxes.

934
00:36:45,220 --> 00:36:46,400
It is they have a dread

935
00:36:46,400 --> 00:36:49,640
of an overweening autocratic monarchy.

936
00:36:50,240 --> 00:36:53,120
So the nobles are very good at posing

937
00:36:53,120 --> 00:36:55,460
as the defenders of the French people's liberties.

938
00:36:56,160 --> 00:36:57,400
And people believe it.

939
00:36:57,580 --> 00:36:59,960
You know, people are roused by it.

940
00:37:00,040 --> 00:37:01,580
But they're kind of riding a crocodile, right?

941
00:37:01,980 --> 00:37:03,140
They are. Oh, totally. Yeah.

942
00:37:03,280 --> 00:37:06,120
The notables are in for a shock.

943
00:37:06,120 --> 00:37:06,420
Bit of a shock.

944
00:37:07,800 --> 00:37:10,200
So now Louis has to find a new finance minister.

945
00:37:10,200 --> 00:37:12,600
And he says, well, what I'll do is

946
00:37:12,600 --> 00:37:15,080
I'll get one of the notables to do it.

947
00:37:15,140 --> 00:37:17,620
I mean, that's quite a canny idea.

948
00:37:18,180 --> 00:37:21,040
He gets a guy who's the Archbishop of Toulouse

949
00:37:21,040 --> 00:37:23,260
who'd been a very outspoken notable

950
00:37:23,260 --> 00:37:26,760
who's called Étienne-Charles de Lumigny de Brienne.

951
00:37:27,080 --> 00:37:28,280
He's one of those kind of classic

952
00:37:28,280 --> 00:37:30,640
late 18th century French bishops

953
00:37:30,640 --> 00:37:34,520
who don't really believe in God or any of that nonsense.

954
00:37:34,820 --> 00:37:37,020
There was talk of making him Archbishop of Paris.

955
00:37:37,180 --> 00:37:38,160
Louis had apparently said,

956
00:37:38,300 --> 00:37:40,180
surely as Archbishop,

957
00:37:40,180 --> 00:37:42,040
he should believe in God, shouldn't he?

958
00:37:42,080 --> 00:37:45,460
And Brienne also, entertainingly,

959
00:37:45,480 --> 00:37:47,520
there's a lot of skin disease in this series.

960
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:48,740
There really is, isn't there?

961
00:37:48,900 --> 00:37:52,280
So Brienne, I read, he suffered from a skin disease

962
00:37:52,280 --> 00:37:53,400
that left his face, quote,

963
00:37:53,440 --> 00:37:55,860
a mass of peeling scabs and tissue.

964
00:37:56,100 --> 00:37:59,360
And yet he was thought of as a personable and congenial man.

965
00:37:59,900 --> 00:38:01,780
So that says a lot for his personality.

966
00:38:02,180 --> 00:38:02,660
It does.

967
00:38:02,700 --> 00:38:03,640
Well, so we've had Louis XV

968
00:38:03,640 --> 00:38:06,040
who died with his face covered in pustules.

969
00:38:06,120 --> 00:38:09,440
And we're going to be meeting Marat, aren't we?

970
00:38:09,580 --> 00:38:09,980
Yeah.

971
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:12,280
Mirabeau, the ugliest man in France.

972
00:38:12,480 --> 00:38:13,100
Mirabeau, yes.

973
00:38:13,260 --> 00:38:13,840
Yes, his face.

974
00:38:13,960 --> 00:38:14,340
Well, yes.

975
00:38:14,600 --> 00:38:17,800
So lots of big figures with unpleasant skin complaints.

976
00:38:18,080 --> 00:38:18,400
Yeah.

977
00:38:19,080 --> 00:38:21,300
So Brienne had been an opposition figure.

978
00:38:22,120 --> 00:38:24,500
And he had said, like a lot of the notables,

979
00:38:24,640 --> 00:38:27,260
I think Callon's stuff about the deficit is balderdash.

980
00:38:27,380 --> 00:38:29,040
I don't think there really is a deficit, actually.

981
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:29,920
That's how I look in the books.

982
00:38:30,040 --> 00:38:31,400
That's how I look in the books.

983
00:38:31,400 --> 00:38:31,500
Oh, my God.

984
00:38:31,820 --> 00:38:32,780
There'll be loads of money.

985
00:38:32,900 --> 00:38:33,880
And he looks at the books.

986
00:38:33,940 --> 00:38:36,400
He says, well, Callon, it's true.

987
00:38:36,540 --> 00:38:38,300
Callon wasn't telling the truth about the deficit

988
00:38:38,300 --> 00:38:39,380
because he wasn't.

989
00:38:39,380 --> 00:38:41,260
It's not 80 million.

990
00:38:41,440 --> 00:38:42,860
It's 140 million.

991
00:38:43,240 --> 00:38:44,140
Oh, God.

992
00:38:44,180 --> 00:38:47,300
So actually, Brienne then has to go back with his skin complaint

993
00:38:47,300 --> 00:38:49,640
to his old chap chums in the notables.

994
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:54,060
And he says, actually, we do need new taxes.

995
00:38:54,180 --> 00:38:57,420
And he wants a land tax and a stamp tax.

996
00:38:57,740 --> 00:39:00,120
What could possibly go wrong with a stamp tax, Tom?

997
00:39:00,460 --> 00:39:02,340
There's always very successful we's.

998
00:39:02,680 --> 00:39:06,940
And the notables say, yet again, no.

999
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:08,160
No, we don't either.

1000
00:39:08,160 --> 00:39:11,840
So you will have to call the Estates General if you want new taxes.

1001
00:39:12,100 --> 00:39:13,720
Whatever it is, you have to call it.

1002
00:39:14,040 --> 00:39:16,420
So they scrapped the assembly of the notables.

1003
00:39:16,460 --> 00:39:17,760
So we're in 1787.

1004
00:39:19,400 --> 00:39:21,920
And Louis is very, very depressed by all this.

1005
00:39:22,040 --> 00:39:26,020
So there are accounts of him saying he's basically spent all his time

1006
00:39:26,020 --> 00:39:29,480
now going hunting and drinking and eating because he realizes.

1007
00:39:29,480 --> 00:39:32,080
I mean, it's amazing that he can go hunting because he's,

1008
00:39:32,100 --> 00:39:33,500
by this point, got incredibly obese.

1009
00:39:34,160 --> 00:39:36,860
So you pity the horse that has to carry him.

1010
00:39:37,460 --> 00:39:38,380
He's getting very fat.

1011
00:39:38,780 --> 00:39:39,660
He is very fat.

1012
00:39:39,700 --> 00:39:40,820
But it's a sad fat, Tom.

1013
00:39:40,920 --> 00:39:41,600
Not a jolly fat.

1014
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:41,880
Oh, definitely.

1015
00:39:42,340 --> 00:39:43,580
He's not a jolly man.

1016
00:39:44,000 --> 00:39:44,400
No.

1017
00:39:44,460 --> 00:39:45,460
He's a sad fat man.

1018
00:39:45,680 --> 00:39:52,540
He's a sad fat man because I think he can see that this is going to be really difficult.

1019
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:54,560
Well, because what options does he have?

1020
00:39:54,800 --> 00:39:56,000
Well, I mean, terrible.

1021
00:39:56,120 --> 00:39:57,040
Every option is bad.

1022
00:39:57,580 --> 00:39:59,900
So Brianne and Louis get together.

1023
00:39:59,940 --> 00:40:00,780
And what are we going to do?

1024
00:40:01,540 --> 00:40:02,520
They have two options.

1025
00:40:02,640 --> 00:40:06,100
One is they could just do what everyone says and call this Estates General.

1026
00:40:07,060 --> 00:40:12,660
But the issue with that is once you open that box, one of Simon Sharma's boxes,

1027
00:40:14,600 --> 00:40:15,460
where's it going to lead?

1028
00:40:15,760 --> 00:40:17,500
Like, will they have to have a constitution?

1029
00:40:18,020 --> 00:40:19,360
Will they demand more power?

1030
00:40:19,460 --> 00:40:22,580
Will you basically be reduced to being a British constitutional monarch?

1031
00:40:22,800 --> 00:40:25,060
And Louis doesn't really want that for obvious reasons.

1032
00:40:25,300 --> 00:40:26,780
I mean, why would you?

1033
00:40:26,840 --> 00:40:28,860
You'd want to keep as much power as possible.

1034
00:40:30,100 --> 00:40:34,520
The other thing they could do is they could go back to the old traditional thing of going through these courts,

1035
00:40:34,600 --> 00:40:35,240
these parliaments.

1036
00:40:35,240 --> 00:40:39,360
And somehow persuade or bully them into approving the new taxes.

1037
00:40:39,540 --> 00:40:41,480
And Brianne says, look, I'll do that.

1038
00:40:41,980 --> 00:40:46,420
So he goes to the Paris parlement, summer of 1787.

1039
00:40:47,600 --> 00:40:49,340
How about a stamp and tax?

1040
00:40:49,580 --> 00:40:50,740
How about a land tax, lads?

1041
00:40:51,280 --> 00:40:54,220
And the parlement of Paris say, no way.

1042
00:40:54,740 --> 00:40:58,200
And they reject them in very groundbreaking language.

1043
00:40:58,480 --> 00:41:02,400
They say, the days when kings just asked for taxes are gone.

1044
00:41:03,160 --> 00:41:04,600
Like, absolutism is over.

1045
00:41:04,600 --> 00:41:06,360
And the king must be governed by the law.

1046
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:12,420
The nation must give its consent to new taxes with regular meetings of the estates general.

1047
00:41:12,620 --> 00:41:18,400
And for the first time, they use a word to describe their fellow Frenchmen that had not been used before by such institutions.

1048
00:41:18,680 --> 00:41:20,200
They don't call them subjects to the king.

1049
00:41:20,320 --> 00:41:22,800
They call them citoyens, citizens.

1050
00:41:23,300 --> 00:41:29,360
But the thing that's amazing about this is that it's in defense of nobles and clergy not paying tax.

1051
00:41:29,660 --> 00:41:30,880
Yeah, it is.

1052
00:41:31,000 --> 00:41:34,580
All this kind of language that will be used in the revolution.

1053
00:41:34,580 --> 00:41:41,880
The revolution that consumes both the church and the nobility is first being used to defend the privileges of the church and the nobility.

1054
00:41:42,060 --> 00:41:42,600
It is.

1055
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:43,380
It's bonkers, isn't it?

1056
00:41:43,440 --> 00:41:44,580
And I think there's two things there.

1057
00:41:44,640 --> 00:41:46,680
One is that there's a degree of self-interest, obviously.

1058
00:41:47,240 --> 00:42:00,060
But the rules are also the idea that all the nobility are like this sort of Dickensian vicomtes who are like whipping their peasants and forcing themselves on sort of poor women and stuff.

1059
00:42:00,460 --> 00:42:02,640
A lot of these people are actually quite liberal.

1060
00:42:03,200 --> 00:42:03,680
Yeah.

1061
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:09,000
They have absorbed the language of the Americans, of the American colonists.

1062
00:42:09,420 --> 00:42:15,640
And they genuinely believe in the Estates General and they actually do like the idea of a British constitutional monarchy.

1063
00:42:15,900 --> 00:42:31,200
And we talked about one of them in the previous episode, Philippe, the Duke of Orléans, whose Palais Real in the center of Paris is the great center of free speech of the pamphlets and will be the center for the Jacobins.

1064
00:42:31,480 --> 00:42:32,360
Yeah, exactly.

1065
00:42:32,360 --> 00:42:53,760
So there are people who genuinely, it's partly self-interest, but there are people, I mean, let's not pretend that there aren't people who genuinely believe this and really do think that this is a great way, that making a huge fuss about these taxes is a great way to get what they want in the long run, which is basically the British system, the system that had been advocated by the great Enlightenment writer, Montesquieu.

1066
00:42:54,620 --> 00:43:00,780
Let's have that in France, separation of powers, a proper legislature, all of that, prime ministers, all of that kind of stuff.

1067
00:43:01,760 --> 00:43:02,200
Anyway.

1068
00:43:02,200 --> 00:43:06,640
Brienne is outraged that the Parliament has rejected his plan.

1069
00:43:07,080 --> 00:43:09,100
And he now does something.

1070
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:12,500
It does a couple of very, very drastic things.

1071
00:43:12,760 --> 00:43:21,900
First of all, the Parliament is exiled to Troyes in August, and literally it is sealed off by the Swiss Guard, by an elite unit of the French army.

1072
00:43:22,320 --> 00:43:24,040
And they're all basically kicked out.

1073
00:43:24,440 --> 00:43:28,120
And the reaction to that in Paris is total bedlam.

1074
00:43:28,200 --> 00:43:31,620
So if you had a time machine, you went to Paris in 1787.

1075
00:43:31,620 --> 00:43:32,440
You got out.

1076
00:43:32,880 --> 00:43:34,940
You would think that you're called.

1077
00:43:35,140 --> 00:43:36,640
Yeah, you got your wrong settings wrong.

1078
00:43:36,700 --> 00:43:43,100
You got the settings wrong because you'd think, well, this is the French Revolution because there is rioting by the clerks.

1079
00:43:43,680 --> 00:43:47,940
People who are giving out royal edicts are attacked by the crowd and two of them almost beaten to death.

1080
00:43:48,260 --> 00:43:52,380
A suspected police agent is beaten to death by the crowd.

1081
00:43:52,920 --> 00:43:58,960
And the government eventually has to send in infantry units to secure the streets of the capital.

1082
00:43:59,140 --> 00:44:00,740
And they end up shutting down.

1083
00:44:01,180 --> 00:44:02,600
Loads of clubs and societies.

1084
00:44:03,060 --> 00:44:08,800
They even shut down chess clubs because they don't want subversive ideas to spread.

1085
00:44:09,380 --> 00:44:16,800
And there's the real sense from 1787 from this point that the capital is, dare I say, a tinderbox, Tom?

1086
00:44:17,260 --> 00:44:18,960
I mean, is it more than a tinderbox?

1087
00:44:19,040 --> 00:44:20,520
Has it already slipped royal control?

1088
00:44:21,760 --> 00:44:24,040
I think to some degree it has slipped royal control, actually.

1089
00:44:24,460 --> 00:44:30,400
And I think from 1787 all the way through to 1789, it's a question of trying to regain royal control.

1090
00:44:30,400 --> 00:44:35,880
And there are troops on the streets, you know, pretty much all the time from this period.

1091
00:44:36,080 --> 00:44:40,520
Because there is the queen being forbidden by the king to go into Paris.

1092
00:44:40,860 --> 00:44:41,040
Right.

1093
00:44:41,100 --> 00:44:41,540
Exactly.

1094
00:44:41,680 --> 00:44:42,140
Exactly.

1095
00:44:43,100 --> 00:44:45,060
So that's against this backdrop, isn't it?

1096
00:44:45,100 --> 00:44:46,580
The hostility to Marie Antoinette.

1097
00:44:47,480 --> 00:44:47,940
Absolutely.

1098
00:44:48,040 --> 00:44:48,180
Yeah.

1099
00:44:49,000 --> 00:44:51,660
So the months pass very uneasily.

1100
00:44:51,720 --> 00:44:57,500
And then in April 1788, Brienne unveils a new plan to try and break the deadlock.

1101
00:44:58,140 --> 00:44:59,800
And it's quite clever in a way.

1102
00:45:00,400 --> 00:45:07,200
What he does is he will strip these courts, the parlement, of their ability to kind of police royal edicts.

1103
00:45:07,660 --> 00:45:11,300
He will beef up the powers of the courts below them, the lower courts.

1104
00:45:11,440 --> 00:45:13,180
They'll take over a lot of the parlement's power.

1105
00:45:13,340 --> 00:45:17,700
And there'll be a new court set up, a plenary court, which will approve royal taxes.

1106
00:45:18,440 --> 00:45:21,640
And obviously he will stuff that court with his people.

1107
00:45:23,080 --> 00:45:29,920
And the parlement will just be reduced to basically arbitrating disputes among the nobility, which is a pretty pitiful kind of thing for them to be doing.

1108
00:45:29,920 --> 00:45:34,260
And again, there are massive riots in Paris.

1109
00:45:34,460 --> 00:45:36,780
There's great outpouring of rage.

1110
00:45:37,060 --> 00:45:43,180
But the interesting thing here, right, so much of the French Revolution story is told about Paris.

1111
00:45:43,420 --> 00:45:48,200
And you just assume the rest of the country is being dragged along in Paris' wake.

1112
00:45:48,740 --> 00:45:57,300
But that's not the case because the worst riots about the Brienne's parlement plan are in places like Lyon and Aix and Dijon.

1113
00:45:57,400 --> 00:45:59,120
So provincial capitals.

1114
00:45:59,120 --> 00:46:02,740
Where having a high court was very important to them.

1115
00:46:02,820 --> 00:46:03,880
I mean, it's not surprising, is it?

1116
00:46:03,880 --> 00:46:10,640
Because we talked about how France is a much more geographically diffuse country than, say, Britain.

1117
00:46:11,820 --> 00:46:17,240
And well, actually, you know, I mean, it's a bit like Scotland defending its distinctiveness against London.

1118
00:46:18,120 --> 00:46:18,600
Exactly.

1119
00:46:18,720 --> 00:46:19,100
Yeah.

1120
00:46:19,200 --> 00:46:22,200
What makes you distinctive is incredibly important to you.

1121
00:46:22,440 --> 00:46:22,920
Yes.

1122
00:46:23,420 --> 00:46:28,240
Imagine the government trying to scrap the courts in Scotland and saying, let's have just one British court.

1123
00:46:28,240 --> 00:46:29,920
I mean, imagine that.

1124
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:32,560
Or the controversial church or whatever, which was.

1125
00:46:32,620 --> 00:46:33,300
Yeah, exactly.

1126
00:46:33,660 --> 00:46:36,620
Now, this brings us to the city where the French Revolution begins.

1127
00:46:36,840 --> 00:46:38,820
And it's an unexpected place, isn't it?

1128
00:46:38,940 --> 00:46:38,960
It is.

1129
00:46:38,960 --> 00:46:41,040
So I don't think the French Revolution begins in 1789.

1130
00:46:41,100 --> 00:46:42,480
I think it begins in 1788.

1131
00:46:42,660 --> 00:46:45,120
And I think it begins in Grenoble.

1132
00:46:45,340 --> 00:46:45,560
Yeah.

1133
00:46:45,620 --> 00:46:46,720
So justify that, Dominic.

1134
00:46:47,740 --> 00:46:53,260
So Grenoble, a city in the foothills of the Alps, southeastern France.

1135
00:46:54,380 --> 00:46:56,780
It calls itself the cradle of the revolution.

1136
00:46:56,780 --> 00:46:59,400
That's how Grenoble brands itself to this day.

1137
00:47:00,600 --> 00:47:05,400
It's a very ordinary place, which you might think makes it a weird place for the revolution to have begun.

1138
00:47:05,520 --> 00:47:08,240
But no, the ordinariness is the point.

1139
00:47:08,360 --> 00:47:11,760
That is precisely why the revolution begins in Grenoble.

1140
00:47:12,240 --> 00:47:18,340
Grenoble is the capital of the province of Dauphine, a long way from Paris.

1141
00:47:18,780 --> 00:47:21,260
Its regional distinctiveness matters to it.

1142
00:47:21,740 --> 00:47:23,660
And there's a parliament in Dauphine.

1143
00:47:23,900 --> 00:47:26,120
And there are loads of people associated with it.

1144
00:47:27,180 --> 00:47:31,800
Lawyers, journalists, useless writers, all of this kind of thing.

1145
00:47:31,840 --> 00:47:32,860
The kind of people you get on Twitter.

1146
00:47:33,640 --> 00:47:34,600
Exactly that.

1147
00:47:34,780 --> 00:47:35,480
Exactly that.

1148
00:47:35,540 --> 00:47:39,440
People who spend all day sitting behind their computers firing off angry messages.

1149
00:47:39,760 --> 00:47:42,340
They owe their livelihoods to that court.

1150
00:47:42,520 --> 00:47:47,080
If it is downgraded or removed, they will lose that.

1151
00:47:49,520 --> 00:47:53,500
Also, Grenoble is a glove-making center.

1152
00:47:54,060 --> 00:47:55,440
And they're very good at making gloves.

1153
00:47:55,440 --> 00:47:57,660
They export them all over the world to America, to Russia.

1154
00:47:58,240 --> 00:48:03,240
But the glove-making business is in real trouble because all of these woes have led to an economic downturn.

1155
00:48:03,280 --> 00:48:04,760
And lots of people have been put out of work.

1156
00:48:04,840 --> 00:48:05,680
So there's a lot of anger.

1157
00:48:07,900 --> 00:48:12,580
So on Saturday, the 7th of June, 1788, the day the French Revolution begins,

1158
00:48:14,140 --> 00:48:17,960
Brienne has his edict read out in the marketplace.

1159
00:48:19,380 --> 00:48:20,260
It's market day.

1160
00:48:20,440 --> 00:48:22,440
And he has troops go to it.

1161
00:48:22,440 --> 00:48:26,040
And he's trying to basically take the parliament leaders out of Grenoble,

1162
00:48:26,120 --> 00:48:29,040
which is what he's trying to do across the kingdom, to take them out of their centers.

1163
00:48:30,060 --> 00:48:34,900
And the law clerks come out and they start haranguing people and shouting at people in the marketplace.

1164
00:48:35,100 --> 00:48:35,840
This is what's happening.

1165
00:48:35,880 --> 00:48:36,480
Isn't this terrible?

1166
00:48:36,820 --> 00:48:42,060
And it basically escalates from a protest to a general strike and then a huge riot.

1167
00:48:42,360 --> 00:48:43,780
They close the city gates.

1168
00:48:43,900 --> 00:48:45,400
They lay siege to the governor's house.

1169
00:48:46,100 --> 00:48:48,760
The authorities send in soldiers, but not enough.

1170
00:48:50,440 --> 00:48:56,360
People go up to the roofs of their houses and they get these tiles and they start bombarding the soldiers with tiles.

1171
00:48:56,520 --> 00:48:58,780
So it goes down in history as the Day of the Tiles.

1172
00:48:59,840 --> 00:49:03,820
The soldiers, as is so predictable, they lose their cool.

1173
00:49:03,880 --> 00:49:04,640
They start shooting.

1174
00:49:04,740 --> 00:49:06,980
They kill a 12-year-old boy and they kill a hatter.

1175
00:49:07,940 --> 00:49:09,660
And then the crowd go ballistic.

1176
00:49:09,760 --> 00:49:13,480
They're parading the bloodied clothes of these people around.

1177
00:49:13,600 --> 00:49:18,300
They start ringing the bells to summon peasants in from the countryside because, of course,

1178
00:49:18,300 --> 00:49:21,680
because it's market day, a lot of people have come in from the countryside already.

1179
00:49:22,100 --> 00:49:24,040
So they're sailing to the peasants.

1180
00:49:24,320 --> 00:49:26,280
Your relatives are under attack from the soldiers.

1181
00:49:26,340 --> 00:49:27,320
Come and defend them quickly.

1182
00:49:27,460 --> 00:49:28,920
So people are pouring into the city.

1183
00:49:30,140 --> 00:49:31,740
Basically, the crowd seizes.

1184
00:49:31,760 --> 00:49:33,520
The mob seizes the city.

1185
00:49:33,660 --> 00:49:35,020
They sack the governor's house.

1186
00:49:35,080 --> 00:49:36,320
Tom, you'll be gutted about this.

1187
00:49:36,560 --> 00:49:40,140
They ransacked the governor's natural history cabinet.

1188
00:49:40,260 --> 00:49:40,940
I saw that.

1189
00:49:41,260 --> 00:49:41,700
Yes.

1190
00:49:41,940 --> 00:49:44,540
And they took a stuffed eagle.

1191
00:49:45,360 --> 00:49:46,200
Yeah, I suppose.

1192
00:49:46,300 --> 00:49:48,280
I mean, I suppose you can't make an omission.

1193
00:49:48,300 --> 00:49:49,140
You can't make an omelette without breaking eggs.

1194
00:49:49,280 --> 00:49:53,900
Well, of course, as famously as has been remarked on this podcast, they do not actually make

1195
00:49:53,900 --> 00:49:54,320
the omelette.

1196
00:49:55,020 --> 00:50:00,720
So basically, there is an intra-elite attempt to kind of grab the levers of power.

1197
00:50:01,260 --> 00:50:06,460
But essentially, that fight between the kind of rival sections of the elite has been overtaken

1198
00:50:06,460 --> 00:50:08,680
by a mass uprising.

1199
00:50:09,040 --> 00:50:09,720
Yeah, it has.

1200
00:50:09,880 --> 00:50:12,880
And actually, Simon Sharma is absolutely brilliant on this.

1201
00:50:12,940 --> 00:50:16,320
It's one of the many, many fantastic insights in his book.

1202
00:50:16,500 --> 00:50:17,720
And we shouldn't make this.

1203
00:50:17,720 --> 00:50:18,620
It's the Sharma fan cast.

1204
00:50:19,680 --> 00:50:24,320
But he points out the judges are carried aloft by the crowd, the parliament judges.

1205
00:50:24,940 --> 00:50:27,040
But he says it's not clear at this point.

1206
00:50:27,120 --> 00:50:29,600
You know, are they the leaders?

1207
00:50:29,680 --> 00:50:30,840
They're not the leaders of the riot.

1208
00:50:31,280 --> 00:50:35,780
They're more like it's kind of its trophies, a bit like the stuffed eagle.

1209
00:50:36,080 --> 00:50:37,840
But they're also kind of its prisoners.

1210
00:50:38,020 --> 00:50:38,400
Right.

1211
00:50:38,480 --> 00:50:40,400
And this is something that will happen again and again.

1212
00:50:41,420 --> 00:50:44,940
So, I mean, looking ahead, it's an experience that the royal family will soon be.

1213
00:50:45,160 --> 00:50:45,560
Exactly.

1214
00:50:46,100 --> 00:50:46,500
Yeah.

1215
00:50:46,500 --> 00:50:51,920
And some of the older judges are very anxious about this and end up actually leaving the city.

1216
00:50:52,040 --> 00:50:53,640
They don't want to be associated with this.

1217
00:50:53,780 --> 00:51:01,820
But there's a younger generation who are enthused and actually see in the disorder and the collapse

1218
00:51:01,820 --> 00:51:08,320
of royal authority a chance to lead, to profit.

1219
00:51:08,680 --> 00:51:13,640
And the classic example of this is a guy who I think is, you can argue, is the first true

1220
00:51:13,640 --> 00:51:15,000
French revolutionary leader.

1221
00:51:15,560 --> 00:51:19,440
So, he's a young judge from Grenoble and he's called Jean-Joseph Meunier.

1222
00:51:19,980 --> 00:51:24,140
And he is just such a perfect example of a French revolutionary leader.

1223
00:51:24,600 --> 00:51:26,120
He's the son of a cloth merchant.

1224
00:51:26,300 --> 00:51:27,220
He's upwardly mobile.

1225
00:51:27,400 --> 00:51:29,940
He's 29, so he's young, as so many of them were.

1226
00:51:30,080 --> 00:51:30,760
And he's a lawyer.

1227
00:51:30,760 --> 00:51:31,060
He's a lawyer.

1228
00:51:31,240 --> 00:51:31,600
Yeah.

1229
00:51:31,700 --> 00:51:32,380
They're all lawyers.

1230
00:51:32,480 --> 00:51:33,280
They're all lawyers, aren't they?

1231
00:51:33,300 --> 00:51:34,020
They're all young lawyers.

1232
00:51:34,480 --> 00:51:35,780
And he's really ambitious.

1233
00:51:36,140 --> 00:51:39,100
They're basically like Athelstan members, Tom.

1234
00:51:39,400 --> 00:51:42,300
They're young lawyers of the Restless History Club.

1235
00:51:43,260 --> 00:51:44,440
French revolutionaries in waiting.

1236
00:51:44,440 --> 00:51:50,500
So, he has bought the office of a royal judge and he has actually become a very, very,

1237
00:51:50,520 --> 00:51:52,920
he's on the lowest possible rank of the nobility.

1238
00:51:53,380 --> 00:51:58,240
And isn't that always the way, classically, in a revolution, that it's the people, it's

1239
00:51:58,240 --> 00:52:03,660
not the poor, it's always the upwardly mobile who haven't got quite upwardly mobile enough?

1240
00:52:03,980 --> 00:52:04,500
Exactly.

1241
00:52:04,660 --> 00:52:05,160
Exactly.

1242
00:52:05,180 --> 00:52:11,000
And he sees this as a chance to jump up the ladder and to turn the ladder into his ladder.

1243
00:52:11,660 --> 00:52:14,340
So, he convenes a huge meeting.

1244
00:52:14,440 --> 00:52:19,700
On the 21st of July, 1788, outside Grenoble, at the Chateau de Wiesel.

1245
00:52:20,260 --> 00:52:24,460
The Chateau de Wiesel, if you go there today, it is the world's only museum of the French

1246
00:52:24,460 --> 00:52:24,820
Revolution.

1247
00:52:25,040 --> 00:52:26,020
And there's a reason for that.

1248
00:52:26,280 --> 00:52:30,640
Because you can argue, again, this is one of the absolute places where the French Revolution

1249
00:52:30,640 --> 00:52:31,260
began.

1250
00:52:31,480 --> 00:52:35,220
Well, particularly if you are the Grenoble Tourist Board.

1251
00:52:35,340 --> 00:52:36,680
If you're the Grenoble Tourist Board, exactly.

1252
00:52:37,560 --> 00:52:41,460
And at this meeting, he says, listen, we want free elections.

1253
00:52:41,460 --> 00:52:46,420
We want the Estates-General of Dauphine, each province to have its own Estates-General.

1254
00:52:46,740 --> 00:52:52,420
And when that's going to meet, and when that meets, the third estate, the Commons, should

1255
00:52:52,420 --> 00:52:56,080
have the same numbers as the other two estates, the Lords and the clergy, put together.

1256
00:52:56,560 --> 00:53:00,900
And they should all sit in one chamber, not multiple chambers like they have in Britain,

1257
00:53:01,100 --> 00:53:05,280
Lords and Commons, or one chamber so that the Commons can dominate.

1258
00:53:05,400 --> 00:53:11,360
In other words, majority rule, the general will, will dominate over the privileged.

1259
00:53:11,360 --> 00:53:12,940
The villages of the old order.

1260
00:53:13,960 --> 00:53:23,000
And a lot of historians say that is so groundbreaking and seismic that that is the true kickoff

1261
00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:28,140
point for the Revolution, the chain of events that will lead to the Revolution.

1262
00:53:28,380 --> 00:53:31,340
There's two other things that I think are really revealing about that meeting at Wiesel.

1263
00:53:31,680 --> 00:53:35,500
First of all, is the language is so 1793.

1264
00:53:36,160 --> 00:53:39,360
It's all about patriotic fire, sacrifices.

1265
00:53:40,200 --> 00:53:41,100
The law.

1266
00:53:41,360 --> 00:53:43,180
It should be the expression of the general will.

1267
00:53:43,840 --> 00:53:46,940
But the other thing is there is a darker side to it.

1268
00:53:47,120 --> 00:53:54,920
So for the first time, these people say our opponents are, we are patriots, but our opponents

1269
00:53:54,920 --> 00:53:59,500
are traitors to the patrie and they should be treated accordingly.

1270
00:54:00,020 --> 00:54:07,260
Because there's a kind of incipiently totalitarian quality to saying that you are the embodiment

1271
00:54:07,260 --> 00:54:08,360
of the nation.

1272
00:54:08,740 --> 00:54:09,500
Definitely there is, Tom.

1273
00:54:09,520 --> 00:54:10,740
Because if you are the embodiment of the nation.

1274
00:54:10,740 --> 00:54:13,000
Your enemies, therefore, aren't.

1275
00:54:13,020 --> 00:54:14,440
And by definition, the traitors.

1276
00:54:14,520 --> 00:54:16,080
Yeah, I totally agree with you.

1277
00:54:16,140 --> 00:54:16,880
I totally agree.

1278
00:54:17,060 --> 00:54:20,820
Now, of course, you can say there's a great irony here that actually even Mounier and

1279
00:54:20,820 --> 00:54:23,760
his friends have not really understood what happened in Grenoble.

